WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.000
<v Mara>Welcome back to Harbor Voices, the show about small ports and the people who keep them running.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:27.000
<v Mara>Today we are at Grey Point with Tobias Renn, who has kept the lighthouse there for 20 years.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:41.000
<v Tobias>Thanks, Mara. The lamp still burns colza oil, about 12 litres a night in the winter months.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:55.000
<v Mara>How did the Grey Point Trust decide to keep the old lamp instead of fitting an electric one?

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:09.000
<v Tobias>The Grey Point Trust voted on it in March, and the old lamp won by 30 votes to nine.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:23.000
<v Tobias>An electric lamp would have cost the Trust 90 thousand dollars before a single night of light.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:37.000
<v Mara>Tobias Renn keeps a ledger of every ship that passes, and the ledger is the reason we came.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:51.000
<v Tobias>The first entry is from 1902, a coal barge called the Wren, and we have 210 volumes now.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:02:05.000
<v Mara>What does the ledger tell you about the harbour that the official port records do not?

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:19.000
<v Tobias>The port records count tonnage, but the ledger records weather, crews and who was late and why.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:33.000
<v Tobias>Last winter the ledger showed 40 ships turning back at the bar, twice the number of the year before.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:47.000
<v Mara>The Harbour Board reportedly disputes that count and says its own radar logs show far fewer.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:03:01.000
<v Tobias>It is unclear whose count is right, because the radar at North Quay was down for most of January.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:15.000
<v Mara>Why does it matter to a fishing family in the town whether the count is forty or eighty?

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:29.000
<v Tobias>Because the dredging budget follows the count, and the Harbour Board sets the dredging budget every spring.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:43.000
<v Tobias>If the bar is not dredged, the larger boats of the Northwind Cooperative cannot land their catch here.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:57.000
<v Mara>The Northwind Cooperative lands about 60 percent of the fish sold at the Saturday market.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:11.000
<v Tobias>I think the town forgets how much of its income crosses that bar in the dark every single night.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:25.000
<v Mara>Let us talk about the keeper's day, because people imagine it as lonely and quiet work.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:39.000
<v Tobias>I trim the wick at dusk, wind the clockwork every four hours, and write the ledger before I sleep.

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:53.000
<v Tobias>The clockwork was built by Halden and Sons in 1911 and it has stopped only three times since then.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:07.000
<v Mara>Could a young person still learn this trade, or does the knowledge end with the keepers we have?

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:21.000
<v Tobias>Two apprentices started with the Trust this year, and one of them already reads the tide tables better than I do.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:35.000
<v Mara>The Grey Point Trust pays for the apprentices out of visitor tickets and a small grant from the county.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:49.000
<v Tobias>We had 9000 visitors last summer, which is more than the lighthouse has ever seen in one season.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:06:03.000
<v Mara>Does the crowd change the place, and is there a point where the Trust would turn people away?

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:17.000
<v Tobias>We cap the tower at twelve people an hour, and nobody climbs after the lamp has been lit.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:31.000
<v Mara>There is a plan from the county to build a visitor centre on the meadow behind the keeper's cottage.

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:45.000
<v Tobias>I am against the visitor centre where it is drawn, because the meadow is where the terns nest in May.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:59.000
<v Mara>Supporters of the plan say the visitor centre would bring thirty jobs to a town that badly needs them.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:13.000
<v Tobias>Both things are true, and I would rather see the centre built at North Quay beside the old customs shed.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:27.000
<v Mara>What would you ask the Harbour Board if you had five minutes in front of all its members?

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:41.000
<v Tobias>I would ask them to publish the radar logs, so that the town can compare them with the ledger.

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:55.000
<v Mara>That is a fair request, and we will put the same question to the Harbour Board ourselves next week.

00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:09.000
<v Tobias>Whatever they answer, the lamp will be lit at dusk, as it has been on every night since 1902.

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:23.000
<v Mara>Tobias Renn, keeper of the Grey Point lighthouse, thank you for showing us the ledger and the lamp.

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:37.000
<v Mara>Next time on Harbor Voices we visit the ice house at North Quay and the family that still runs it.
